<Base
  title="How It Started"
  description="The story of Project Cognify began with a simple question about focus and grew into an ongoing student research effort into how and why teenagers pay attention."
>
  <h1>How It Started</h1>
  <p class="label">How Project Cognify began</p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Where the Question Came From</h2>
    <p>
      One night Jonathan and Jeremy stayed up until 2 AM playing a newly
      released Roblox update, mostly because neither of them wanted to log off
      and risk waking up to spoilers all over their group chat. The next day,
      Jonathan couldn't stop thinking about it: he could focus on that for
      hours without trying, but thirty minutes of homework felt like a fight.
    </p>
    <p>
      It felt like a small, kind of dumb observation at first. Then he started
      asking friends if they felt the same way, and almost everyone said yes.
      So instead of just assuming he knew why, he figured it was worth actually
      looking into.
    </p>

    <h2>Turning an Idea into a Team</h2>
    <p>
      Jonathan had worked with Jeremy and Ryan on a game jam the year before —
      they didn't think about problems the same way, but they worked well
      together and didn't just agree with each other to be nice. When Jonathan
      brought up the question, nobody asked what app they should build. Ryan
      just asked what they actually knew about the problem.
    </p>
    <p>The honest answer was: not much.</p>
    <p>
      So instead of building anything right away, the three of them decided to
      start with research. That decision is basically the origin story of this
      whole project.
    </p>

    <h2>Following the Evidence</h2>
    <p>
      Our first real hypothesis leaned on FOMO — the idea that the same fear of
      missing out that keeps people glued to games could be pointed at
      studying instead. We didn't want to just build around that hunch, though,
      so we went and read the academic literature, ran a survey, and did a
      round of interviews.
    </p>
    <p>
      The research didn't back up the FOMO idea nearly as much as we expected.
      What kept showing up instead was motivation and something closer to
      social presence — the sense that other people are around and doing the
      same thing you are. That's the direction our work has been pointed in
      since.
    </p>

    <h2>Building While Still Learning</h2>
    <p>
      We've tried not to treat research and building as separate phases. Each
      interview or survey response tends to nudge the next version of our
      thinking a little. <a href="/cohort">Cohort</a>, our first prototype, is
      a product of that — it's not there to prove we were right, it's there to
      test whether the presence idea actually holds up. If it doesn't, we'll
      go back and change it.
    </p>

    <h2>Why We're Putting All of This Online</h2>
    <p>
      Most student projects only show up once, as a finished thing. We'd
      rather show the parts most people skip — the surveys that didn't go
      anywhere, the assumption we had to drop, the version of the idea we
      almost built before the research talked us out of it. If Cohort works,
      great. If it doesn't, we still think there's something worth sharing in
      how we got there.
    </p>
    <p>
      We're still three high schoolers figuring this out as we go, and we
      expect a lot of this to keep changing. Thanks for reading this far.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

